    "q-92142": {
      "dom_id": "step-q92142",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14129811/assets/0c9a3175cf873ca57adb7f57494f2288c898af1fab9e705d9baaa5335523fa3e.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the division problem above?",
      "worked_solution": "We have $\\frac{1}{3}$ on the left and $\\frac{1}{12}$ on the right.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14129811/assets/9638292c507e0714051d52053868717b38ca96ecbc2f7f7629eecf3ed9269fc6.png)\n\nThe division operation splits the shaded region on the left into $4$ equal parts. So the shaded part on the left is $4$ times larger than the shaded part on the right. \n\nTherefore, the division problem shown is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}÷4=\\frac{1}{12}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
